# q08 — an interface from an imported library used as a value type, as a
# struct field type, and as a bound; and its defaults reached in a file
# that never writes its name.
#
# Probes: `[Panel]` holding a `Str` and an `(Int, Int)` together, both
# conforming only through an `extend` that arrived with the import; a
# struct field typed `Panel`; `frame.widest[P: Panel]` as a bound two
# files from the `extend`. The word `Panel` does not appear in this file
# at all, yet `"hello".plate` and `pair.big?` must work.
#
# Two things I could not pin down and am guessing at:
#   1. whether an interface is allowed as a struct field type at all —
#      `gallery/wall.lume` writes `hero: Panel`. The README says behaviour
#      "cannot be stored in a field"; that sentence is about blocks, and I
#      am reading it as not covering interface values. If it does cover
#      them, the error belongs at `gallery/wall.lume:10`.
#   2. whether `rest: [Panel]` raises the "holds values of different types
#      behind `Panel`" warning that `examples/interfaces.lume` shows on a
#      `[Shape]` binding, and if so where it points. The EXPECTED OUTPUT
#      below is stdout only and does NOT include any such warning.
#
# Meant to RUN.
#
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
# studio
# 12x9 (108 cm2)
# text: hi (20 cm2)
# 4x4 (16 cm2)
# 144
# 100
# text: hello (50 cm2)
# false

import gallery.wall
import gallery.frame

def main:
  w = wall.hang("studio", (12, 9), ["hi", (4, 4)])
  puts w.title
  for l in w.lines:
    puts l
  puts w.total

  # the same interface as a bound, over a list of one concrete type
  puts frame.widest(["abcdefghij", "xy"])

  # the defaults, straight on built-ins, in a file naming no interface
  puts "hello".plate
  pair = (3, 5)
  puts pair.big?
